   "source": [
    "# Flood Prediction Capstone — Modeling and Evaluation\n",
    "### Predicting next-day flood labels using three-day sliding windows (SW90 and SW17)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Research question:** *Can observations from the previous three days predict whether a flood will occur at station SW90 within the following 24 hours?*\n",
    "\n",
    "**Main approach.** We start with SW90 (Bahadurabad), then add measurements from SW17 (Chilmari). We compare Logistic Regression and Random Forest against a simple **seasonal baseline**: predict a flood during **June, July and August**, and no flood during all other months. The label being predicted always belongs to **SW90**; SW17 provides **input measurements only**.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sliding-window design (the central idea of the project):**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Input window | Target | What the model is allowed to see |\n",
    "|:--|:--|:--|\n",
    "| Day 1 + Day 2 + Day 3 | Flood on Day 4 (0 / 1) | Rainfall, soil moisture, river discharge for Days 1–3; never Day 4 measurements |\n",
    "| Day 2 + Day 3 + Day 4 | Flood on Day 5 (0 / 1) | The window moves forward by one day |\n",
    "\n",
    "A typical chunk has **9 features** with SW90 alone (3 variables × 3 days), or **18 features** with SW90 + SW17 (6 variables × 3 days). Each row of the resulting dataset is **one three-day window**, not one independent daily measurement.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Target-timing assumption:** This notebook uses the value of `flood_within_24h` on *Day 3* as the label for *Day 4*. That is appropriate only if the source's `flood_within_24h` means a forward-looking outcome over the next 24 hours from the Day 3 observation. This definition and the measurement cut-off time must be verified before claiming operational next-day forecasting.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Evaluation:** We omit 2025, retain chronological order, and use the oldest 70% of windows for training and the latest 30% for testing. An exploratory validation split is introduced in the middle of the notebook for comparing options without continually selecting on the test set. The positive flood class is rare, so **flood Precision, Recall, F1 and the Confusion Matrix** matter more than Accuracy alone.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to read this notebook:** Each code cell is preceded by a short explanation of the objective and, where available, interpretation of the saved output. The original code and its outputs have been retained. Later experiments are exploratory and should not be mistaken for a new independent final test.\n"
   ]
  },
