   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "TgpisQGqYmia"
   },
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 12. Final results and interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "### Main test-set comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "All rows below refer to the **same 438 chronological test windows** (431 negatives, **7 positive flood labels**). The values reflect the saved notebook outputs.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Approach | Test Accuracy | Flood Precision | Flood Recall | Flood F1 | Floods detected | False alarms |\n",
    "|:--|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|\n",
    "| **Seasonal baseline: June–August = Flood** | 79.68% | 5.43% | **71.43%** | 0.101 | **5 / 7** | 87 |\n",
    "| **Logistic Regression — SW90** | **98.63%** | 66.67% | 28.57% | **0.400** | 2 / 7 | 1 |\n",
    "| Logistic Regression — SW90 + SW17 | 97.95% | 25.00% | 14.29% | 0.182 | 1 / 7 | 3 |\n",
    "| Random Forest — SW90 + SW17, `max_depth=5` | **98.63%** | **100%*** | 14.29% | 0.250 | 1 / 7 | **0** |\n",
    "\n",
    "*The Random Forest's 100% flood precision is based on **one** positive prediction only and should not be overinterpreted.*\n",
    "\n",
    "### What is the main finding?\n",
    "\n",
    "**The best model by flood F1-score is the SW90-only Logistic Regression (0.400).** It improves substantially over the seasonal baseline's F1 (0.101) and uses three-day environmental measurements instead of a calendar rule. The depth-limited Random Forest also beats the seasonal rule **on F1**, but recognizes only one of seven recorded flood days. **No model beats the seasonal baseline on flood Recall:** the baseline detects five flood-labeled days, while the strongest learned model detects two. Consequently, there is **no single universal winner**; the answer depends on whether the priority is overall precision and fewer false alarms or detecting as many flood cases as possible.\n",
    "\n",
    "A useful additional reference is the **always-NO-FLOOD** rule: it would achieve **431 / 438 = 98.40% Accuracy** on this imbalanced test set, but **0% Recall for floods**. This illustrates why Accuracy by itself can be misleading. The trained models offer a more informative compromise in Precision and F1 but remain weak flood detectors.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Did SW17 help?\n",
    "\n",
    "SW17 provided meaningful additional measurements in some periods: on the **2023 internal validation split**, the full SW90 + SW17 Logistic Regression detected **5 / 11** positive days, compared with **1 / 11** for SW90 alone. Four of those five detections came from **four consecutive dates during June 2023**, potentially a single flood event. Yet on the **later test period**, SW90 + SW17 detected **1 / 7**, compared with **2 / 7** for SW90 alone. **Conclusion:** a second station is a plausible source of predictive information, but the observed gain is **not stable across time**. Adding more features is not automatically better.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Did a deeper or more complex Random Forest help?\n",
    "\n",
    "The default Random Forest fit the training data perfectly (**100% Train Accuracy**) but detected just **1 / 7** positive days on the test set. Limiting tree depth to 5 reduced train accuracy and eliminated two test false alarms, **without improving flood Recall**. Adding three upstream-rainfall features (21 total rather than 18) did not change any of the 438 test **binary predictions**. This suggests that further model complexity or feature accumulation is not the most urgent next step.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Important methodological limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Only seven positive test days.** A single event changes Recall by 14.3 percentage points; these scores are statistically unstable and not strong evidence of future warning performance.\n",
    "2. **Unbalanced classes.** High Accuracy may reflect correct predictions of the majority no-flood class instead of meaningful flood detection. Flood Recall, Precision, F1, confusion matrices and false-alarm counts must be reported together.\n",
    "3. **Dependent sliding windows.** Consecutive three-day chunks overlap, and several labeled flood days may belong to one continuous event. The number of *distinct events* detected may be smaller than the number of positive days detected.\n",
    "4. **Repeated use of the test set.** Different model variants and settings (`max_depth`, additional features) were explored after viewing test outcomes. As a result, comparisons involving those choices are **exploratory** and the test numbers are **not an untouched final estimate** of generalization. A future evaluation should fix the modeling decisions on earlier chronological validation data and use a new, unseen later period once.\n",
    "5. **Uncertain target definition and source alignment.** The procedure assumes that `flood_within_24h` on the final input day refers to the next 24 hours, and that Day 3 measurements are available when the forecast would be issued. That assumption needs source/time-stamp verification for real deployment.\n",
    "6. **Label quality is not established by disagreement.** The March 2024 and July 2024 cases are *review flags*, not corrected labels. Independent flooding reports may apply to a wider geographical area or a different definition than the dataset's station-level label. The original target was deliberately kept unchanged.\n",
    "7. **Not an operational early-warning system.** With 2/7 or fewer positive days detected by the learned models, the current results are insufficient for safety-critical flood alerts.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Presentation-ready conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "> **This project investigated whether floods at SW90 could be predicted one day ahead using the previous three days of environmental measurements.** I constructed chronological sliding-window datasets and tested Logistic Regression and Random Forest using SW90 alone and in combination with SW17. I compared them against a simple seasonal baseline that predicts floods during June, July and August.\n",
    ">\n",
    "> **The SW90-only Logistic Regression achieved the best flood F1-score (0.40), compared with 0.10 for the baseline**, while the baseline achieved higher flood Recall (71% versus 29%) at the cost of many false alarms. Additional SW17 measurements improved performance during one validation period but did not generalize to the later test set, and adding upstream rainfall did not change the Random Forest's binary test predictions.\n",
    ">\n",
    "> **The main lesson is that high Accuracy is not enough for rare-event prediction.** Even though the learned models improved the balance of precision and recall compared with the seasonal rule, they still missed most recorded flood days. Small numbers of flood examples, overlapping windows and possible uncertainty in some labels limit the conclusions. The project demonstrates a clear, reproducible workflow—from window-based feature engineering and chronological splitting to baseline comparison and critical error analysis—while identifying concrete directions for future improvement.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Reasonable next steps (if the project is extended)\n",
    "\n",
    "- Validate the exact target timing and available observation cut-off times; cross-check uncertain labels with independent station-level observations where possible.\n",
    "- Use **walk-forward / multiple chronological validation periods**, then reserve a genuinely later untouched test period for a final report.\n",
    "- Report **event-based** flood detection (number of distinct flood episodes flagged) alongside daily Precision, Recall and F1.\n",
    "- Explore richer *past-only* predictors or additional stations only if they plausibly supply new information, and judge them on validation rather than repeatedly choosing by the same test scores.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Data and contextual references\n",
    "\n",
    "- [Project dataset / cleaned CSV](https://github.com/Burzo572/dsb_project)\n",
    "- [Bangladesh flood dataset documentation](https://github.com/Srabon-bot/Bangladesh-flood-data-set)\n",
    "- [Bangladesh Sangbad Sangstha (BSS), July 4, 2024 — flooding in Jamalpur](https://www.bssnews.net/district/198417)\n",
    "- [Bangladesh Sangbad Sangstha (BSS), July 5, 2024 — flood conditions and Bahadurabad water level](https://www.bssnews.net/district/198642)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Scope of claims:** The notebook's data metrics come from the saved model outputs; the BSS links provide contextual flood reports. Neither a model–label disagreement nor a high river-discharge value, by itself, establishes a mislabeled flood record.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 13. Flood prediction within 48 hours\n",
    "### Prepare the new 48-hour target\n",
    "**Purpose:** Extend the earlier experiment from 24 hours to 48 hours. We keep the same three-day sliding windows and measurements from SW90 and SW17, but use SW90's `flood_within_48h` label. This asks whether a flood is labeled in the next two days, not exactly on Day 5.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": [
    "# Copy our existing 3-day chunks\n",
    "chunks_48h = sw90_sw17_chunks.drop(columns=[\"flood_tomorrow\"]).copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Get 48h flood labels from SW90\n",
    "labels_48h = sw90.set_index(\"date\")[\"flood_within_48h\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Match the label to the last day of each chunk\n",
    "chunks_48h[\"flood_next_48h\"] = (chunks_48h[\"target_date\"] - pd.Timedelta(days=1)).map(labels_48h)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Check results\n",
    "print(\"Shape:\", chunks_48h.shape)\n",
    "print(chunks_48h[\"flood_next_48h\"].value_counts())"
   ],
